# Scenario for scripts/record-scenario.py: SCRT (Standard Call and
# Return Technique), the COSMAC convention Mandelbrot's every function
# call relies on -- there's no hardware CALL instruction or stack, so
# a call is 'SEP R5' followed by the subroutine's address as two
# inline bytes, not code: R5 is a dedicated register pointing at a
# shared routine (scrt_call) that reads those two bytes for itself.
#
# ADDRESS IS PINNED TO THE CURRENT samples/cdp1802/mandelbrot.hex:
# scrt_call is the shared call routine at 002C (scrt.inc). If
# mandelbrot.asm/.inc/scrt.inc/.hex ever change, re-check it against
# the fresh .lst before recording -- a stale address will look like it
# works (it disassembles *something*) while showing the wrong thing.

key ?
pace 2.0

key R
upload samples/cdp1802/mandelbrot.hex

# scrt_call itself, disassembled: ordinary code, safe to read linearly
# (unlike a call site, where the two bytes right after SEP R5 are data,
# not the next instruction -- disassembling across *that* boundary
# would decode the embedded address as garbage instructions instead).
# PHI 15/SEX 2/GLO 4/STXD/GHI 4/STXD push the old link register; the
# LDA/PHI/LDA/PLO pair after that reads the caller's embedded address,
# a byte at a time, into R3 -- that's the whole mechanism, right there.
type D
wait Disassemble?
command 002C 15
pace 2.0

# Run until the chip reaches the interesting part, then step through it:
# LDA/PHI/LDA/PLO reads the caller's embedded address, a byte at a
# time, straight into R3 (watch it appear there mid-sequence); GHI 15
# restores D; BR loops back to SEP R3, which jumps to what R3 now
# holds -- the call finally lands on the subroutine it was headed for.
type g
wait Go until?
command 0037 5
pace 0.9
step 7
pace 1.5

# Watch one frame render -- a half frame measured at ~133s on real
# hardware, hence the long wait.
run 1 400
